11. CONCURRENT EDITING AND CONFLICT RESOLUTION
==================================================

Multiple users should be able to edit different parts of the same world simultaneously.

Handle conflicts safely.

Examples:

- Two users place components in different cells.
- Two users modify different objects.
- Two users edit the same component.
- One user deletes a component while another edits it.
- One user moves a group while another modifies one of its children.
- Two users modify the same wireless channel configuration.
- A user submits an edit based on an outdated world revision.

Requirements:

- Preserve unrelated concurrent edits.
- Detect conflicting modifications.
- Prevent duplicate objects.
- Prevent stale state from silently overwriting newer state.
- Preserve group integrity.
- Preserve component identities.
- Preserve overlapping wire variations.
- Communicate rejected conflicting operations to the client.

Use server-authoritative ordering, revision checks, operation validation, or another suitable strategy.

Do not implement a complex CRDT unless the existing editing model actually benefits from it.

For a first reliable version, server-authoritative mutations with revision checks are acceptable.

